# minimum required Qore version
%requires qore >= 1.19
# assume local scope for variables, do not use "$" signs
%modern
# require type definitions everywhere
# strict argument handling
# enable all warnings

%requires Util
# for non-blocking FTP retrieval; FtpClientIo requires nothing from this module, so there is no cycle
%requires FtpClientIo

module FileLocationHandler {
    version = "3.0";
    desc = "user module for reading and writing file data based on URL-like location strings";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
    init = sub () {
        # setup default handlers
        FileLocationHandler::init();
    };
}

/** @mainpage FileLocationHandler Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section filelocationhandlerintro Introduction to the FileLocationHandler Module

    The %FileLocationHandler module provides an API for returning file data based on a URL-like location string.

    To retrieve file data, call one of:
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::getTextFileFromLocation() "FileLocationHandler::getTextFileFromLocation()"
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::getBinaryFileFromLocation() "FileLocationHandler::getBinaryFileFromLocation()"
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::getStreamReaderFromLocation() "FileLocationHandler::getStreamReaderFromLocation()"
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::getBinaryStreamFromLocation() "FileLocationHandler::getBinaryStreamFromLocation()"
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::getIoPollerForLocation() "FileLocationHandler::getIoPollerForLocation()"
      to read without blocking a thread; see @ref filelocationhandler_nonblocking

    To write file data, call one of:
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::writeFileToLocation() "FileLocationHandler::writeFileToLocation()"
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::getOutputStreamForLocation() "FileLocationHandler::getOutputStreamForLocation()"

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# every call below takes the same kind of location string, whatever the transport behind it
string text = FileLocationHandler::getTextFileFromLocation("file:///var/data/orders.csv");
binary data = FileLocationHandler::getBinaryFileFromLocation("sftp://user@host/outbound/orders.csv");
FileLocationHandler::writeFileToLocation("conn://prod-s3/reports/2026-08.csv", text);
    @endcode

    To register a location handler based on a scheme, call one of:
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::registerHandler() "FileLocationHandler::registerHandler()"
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::tryRegisterHandler() "FileLocationHandler::tryRegisterHandler()"

    This module provides the following classes:
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::AbstractFileLocationHandler "AbstractFileLocationHandler": the abstract API class
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler "FileLocationHandler": the main API class
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerConnection "FileLocationHandlerConnection": class handling
      \c "conn://" and \c "connection://" locations (a named connection plus a path)
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerData "FileLocationHandlerData": class handling \c "data://"
      locations (immedidate data in the location URL)
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerDataProvider "FileLocationHandlerDataProvider": class driving an
      application's declared file API for \c "conn://" locations; not registered for a scheme of its own
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerFile "FileLocationHandlerFile": class handling \c "file://"
      locations (the default if no scheme is present in the location string)
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerFtp "FileLocationHandlerFtp": class handling \c "ftp://" and
      \c "ftps://" locations
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerHttp "FileLocationHandlerHttp": class handling \c "http://" and
      \c "https://" locations
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerRest "FileLocationHandlerRest": class handling \c "rest://" and
      \c "rests://" locations
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerSftp "FileLocationHandlerSftp": class handling \c "sftp://"
      locations
    - @ref FileLocationHandler::OutputStreamWrapper "OutputStreamWrapper": output stream wrapper class for handling
      background I/O operations

    @section filelocationhandler_env FileLocationHandler Environment variable

    The \c QORE_FILE_LOCATION_HANDLERS environment variable will be read to initialize modules that provide support
    for custom URL schemes; multiple modules can be separated by the OS-specific path separator (\c : on Linux / UNIX,
    \c ; on Windows).

    Each module listed in this environment variable should call either
    @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::registerHandler() "FileLocationHandler::registerHandler()" or
    @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::tryRegisterHandler() "FileLocationHandler::tryRegisterHandler()" in
    its \c init code to register a handler for a new scheme.

    @section filelocationhandler_conn Named Connection Locations

    Locations with the \c conn or \c connection scheme name a connection and a path:
    @verbatim
conn://<connection-name>/<path>[{<options>}]
    @endverbatim

    The path is always relative to the connection's own base location, so the connection supplies the host, the
    credentials, and the root, while the location string supplies only the leaf.  This keeps credentials out of
    location strings and lets any API that accepts a file location reuse a configured, monitored connection.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
string csv = FileLocationHandler::getTextFileFromLocation("conn://prod-sftp/outbound/orders.csv");
    @endcode

    Connections are resolved through the \c ConnectionProvider module, which is loaded dynamically on first use;
    this module declares no dependency on it.  The \c QORE_CONNECTION_PROVIDERS environment variable must name at
    least one connection provider module.

    @subsection filelocationhandler_conn_resolution How a Named Connection Is Resolved

    A connection can serve file locations in one of two ways; they are tried in this order:

    |!Order|!Mechanism|!Used by
    |1|The connection returns its own handler from \c ConnectionProvider::AbstractConnection::getFileLocationHandler()|Connections that can stream or poll natively
    |2|The connection's application declares a \c DataProvider::DataProviderFileApiInfo file API, which is driven generically by @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerDataProvider "FileLocationHandlerDataProvider"|Any application that declares one

    A connection matching neither raises \c LOCATION-ERROR naming the connection and its type, so an unsupported
    connection fails with a clear message rather than silently returning nothing.

    The first mechanism takes precedence because it can do more: a native handler can stream and can support
    non-blocking reads, whereas the declarative file API is request/response based — streams are synthesized by
    transferring the whole file in a background thread, and
    @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::getIoPollerForLocation() "getIoPollerForLocation()" is not
    supported through it.

    @par Example: cloud storage through a named connection
    @code{.py}
# the same code reads from Amazon S3, Google Drive, or Dropbox; only the connection name changes
string report = FileLocationHandler::getTextFileFromLocation("conn://prod-s3/reports/2026-08.csv");
string doc = FileLocationHandler::getTextFileFromLocation("conn://team-drive/handbook.md");
string sheet = FileLocationHandler::getTextFileFromLocation("conn://ops-dropbox/inbox/orders.csv");

# writing works the same way
FileLocationHandler::writeFileToLocation("conn://prod-s3/reports/2026-09.csv", report);
    @endcode

    @par Example: passing handler options
    @code{.py}
# options are given in braces after the path and are passed to the resolved handler
string csv = FileLocationHandler::getTextFileFromLocation("conn://prod-sftp/orders.csv{encoding=ISO-8859-1}");
    @endcode

    @section filelocationhandler_nonblocking Non-Blocking Reads

    @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::getIoPollerForLocation() "getIoPollerForLocation()" returns an
    @ref Qore::AbstractPollOperation "AbstractPollOperation" that reads the file without holding a thread blocked on
    I/O.  No I/O is performed until the operation is driven, so the poller can be created cheaply and handed to an
    event loop or to the async I/O controller.

    Supported by \c "file://", \c "data://", \c "http://", \c "https://", \c "rest://", \c "rests://", \c "ftp://",
    \c "ftps://", and \c "sftp://" locations, and by \c conn:// locations resolving to a connection that provides its
    own handler.  \c "sftp://" requires the \c ssh2 module to provide \c SFTPClient::startPollGetFile().

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
AbstractPollOperation op = FileLocationHandler::getIoPollerForLocation("ftp://user:pass@host/orders.csv");
while (!op.goalReached()) {
    *hash<SocketPollInfo> info = op.continuePoll();
    if (!info) {
        break;
    }
    # wait for the descriptor to become ready; an event loop would register it instead
    Socket::poll((info,), 30s);
}
binary contents = op.getOutput();
    @endcode

    @note Handlers that cannot support non-blocking reads throw \c UNIMPLEMENTED with a message saying why, so the
    caller can fall back to a blocking read.

    @note Location strings are frequently derived from user or partner data, and a named connection can reach any
    configured system.  Applications accepting untrusted location strings should restrict which connections are
    reachable with
    @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandler::setConnectionFilter() "FileLocationHandler::setConnectionFilter()"
    or the \c QORE_FILE_LOCATION_CONNECTIONS environment variable.

    @par Example: restricting which connections a location string may reach
    @code{.py}
# the filter receives the connection name and a write flag, and returns True if the use is allowed;
# here partner uploads may only be written to one connection, and nothing may be read through it
FileLocationHandler::setConnectionFilter(bool sub (string name, bool write) {
    return write ? name == "partner-inbox" : name =~ /^prod-/;
});
    @endcode

    If no filter is set in code, \c QORE_FILE_LOCATION_CONNECTIONS is used instead; when set, it lists the allowed
    connection names separated by @ref Qore::PathSep "PathSep" characters.  If neither is set, all connections are
    allowed.

    @section filelocationhandler_relnotes Release Notes

    @subsection filelocationhandler_v3_0 FileLocationHandler v3.0
    - implemented support for non-blocking I/O from file handlers
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4764">issue 4764</a>)
    - \c "sftp://" locations (and \c conn:// locations resolving to them) support non-blocking reads with
      @ref FileLocationHandler::AbstractFileLocationHandler::getIoPollerForLocation()
      "getIoPollerForLocation()"; requires the \c ssh2 module with
      \c SFTPClient::startPollGetFile() (ssh2 2.0+)
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5386">issue 5386</a>)
    - \c "ftp://" and \c "ftps://" locations (and \c conn:// locations resolving to them) support non-blocking reads
      with @ref FileLocationHandler::AbstractFileLocationHandler::getIoPollerForLocation()
      "getIoPollerForLocation()"
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5386">issue 5386</a>)
    - added support for \c conn:// and \c connection:// locations naming a connection and a path
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5386">issue 5386</a>)
    - added @ref FileLocationHandler::FileLocationHandlerDataProvider "FileLocationHandlerDataProvider", so that any
      application declaring a \c DataProvider::DataProviderFileApiInfo file API can serve \c conn:// locations
      generically; connections that can stream or poll natively supply their own handler instead, which takes
      precedence (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5386">issue 5386</a>)
    - added @ref FileLocationHandler::AbstractFileLocationHandler::selfValidatesOptions()
      "AbstractFileLocationHandler::selfValidatesOptions()" so that handlers that cannot know their effective
      option set until the location is resolved can validate options themselves
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5386">issue 5386</a>)

    @subsection filelocationhandler_v2_2_1 FileLocationHandler v2.2.1
    - allow handler information to be returned
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4726">issue 4726</a>)

    @subsection filelocationhandler_v2_2 FileLocationHandler v2.2
    - added support for \c rest:// and \c rests:// locations
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4669">issue 4669</a>)
    - added support for runtime extensibility from environment variables
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4667">issue 4667</a>)

    @subsection filelocationhandler_v2_1 FileLocationHandler v2.1
    - added support for immediate <tt>data://</tt> locations
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4626">issue 4626</a>)

    @subsection filelocationhandler_v2_0 FileLocationHandler v2.0
    - updated for read/write operations and added streaming APIs
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4424">issue 4424</a>)

    @subsection filelocationhandler_v1_0 FileLocationHandler v1.0
    - initial release of the module
*/

#! Contains all public definitions in the FileLocationHandler module
public namespace FileLocationHandler {
}
